  "notes": "Norwegian track-first pop-R&B, 2001–2010; the band is \"88–100 straight (backbeat 2&4) plus 122–130 club\", and the R&B band is the base with the mode at 88, the sourced tempo of the transcribed record. The drum claim is a negative one and it is the whole delta: \"even, unswung programming — kick on 1 and the 8th before 3, snare/clap flat on 2&4, continuous 16th hats, and a **modern 808 drum kit used as contrast against acoustic instruments** rather than as the whole bed\". So `swing.amount` is 0.50, `quantizeStrength` 0.96, `velocityVar` 0.09 and `offGridMs` 0 — the most even kit among the producers here, in a genre whose own note says the rule is *not* to tighten it — with `lockTo808` at 0.4 and a 0.7 hat fill density. Harmony is the sourced template: `I–V–ii–IV` (B♭5–F5–Cm7–E♭6/9) in B♭ **major** at 88, built on \"a **gently strummed acoustic guitar**\", with \"cellos as countermelody\". So the major families lead against a minor-leaning lane, the guitar is the timbre hint, and the counter is at −12 with `sustain_pad` leading — the cellos sit under, not over. The documented workflow (\"instrumental first, topline writer after\"; melodic core and \"killer hooks\" prioritised) is why `chordToneBias` is 0.88, the highest among the producers here: the topline is written to the track's changes. The low end is \"808 sub on chord roots, short and clean; cellos frequently occupy the low-mid instead of a bass riff\" — a plain root part on `mirror_kick`, with no `drums.bass808` block, since the 808 here is a kit choice rather than the bass voice. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is part-sourced: the entry names `Bb`, `F#`, `Ab`, and the remaining values `Eb`, `Bm`, together with all 5 weights, are INFERRED rather than read from the entry.",
